# LINE-ENDING POLICY FOR THIS DIRECTORY -- pinned, not inherited.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. The artifacts here are measurement records whose claim is
# byte-reproducibility: two runs of a generator must produce identical bytes, and a grader
# must be able to diff a re-run against the committed record. Python's Path.write_text()
# applies PLATFORM newline translation unless told otherwise, so the same measurement wrote
# LF bytes on Linux and CRLF bytes on Windows. 30 of the 40 files in this directory ended up
# holding CRLF against LF blobs in HEAD, and `git status` reported them clean -- not because
# they matched, but because git's stat cache had not been invalidated and nothing had looked.
#
# "Clean because nothing has looked" is not clean. Two things fix it and both are needed:
#   1. the generator writes with an explicit newline policy (newline="\n"), and
#   2. git is told the policy here, so no checkout, clone or autocrlf setting can reintroduce
#      the translation on another machine.
#
# dual_denominator_remeasure.py additionally verifies the guarded artifacts byte-for-byte
# against their HEAD blobs on every run, consulting the object store rather than the stat
# cache, so a regression is caught by the generator and not by a later grader.
*.json  text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
